  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "\\(\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt5}\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3-\\sqrt5\\)",
    "B": "\\(4-\\sqrt2-\\sqrt3\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt6-5\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac12(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt5-\\sqrt3)\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac13(\\sqrt3+\\sqrt5-\\sqrt2)\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Group \\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\) as one quantity. Changing the sign of \\(\\sqrt5\\) then creates a difference of squares, which removes that radical from the product:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt5)(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3-\\sqrt5)\\\\\n&\\qquad=(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3)^2-5\\\\\n&\\qquad=2+2\\sqrt6+3-5=2\\sqrt6.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt5>0\\), divide this identity by that quantity to obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt5}\n=\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3-\\sqrt5.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice A.",
